course: dictionary
lesson: 498
title: Many meanings, one short word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The same short word can mean very different things. Context tells you which meaning is right. «There is not one sign language for the whole world: Britain has BSL, America has ASL» shows that ASL is a language, used with the hands, from America.
explain: ASL stands for American Sign Language. People use hand signs, facial expressions and body movements to talk. It is a real language with its own grammar, so it is not just English words made with the hands. «American Sign Language (ASL) and BSL are different languages» means someone who knows BSL may not understand ASL.
explain: Online, asl means something very different. In a chat, «asl?» asks for your age, sex and location, the place where you live. Strangers use it to find out about you. The clues matter: a lesson about sign language is about hands and faces; a stranger asking about your age and home is about your personal details.
explain: The safety rule is simple. If a stranger asks «asl?», or asks where you live or how old you are, do not answer. Tell a trusted adult straight away. Your age, home and personal details are private.
fact: ASL stands for American Sign Language.
fact: ASL is used by many Deaf people in the USA and parts of Canada.
fact: ASL is a real language with its own grammar, and it is different from BSL and Auslan.
fact: «BSL fingerspelling uses two hands, ASL fingerspelling uses one» shows that sign languages differ in small details too.
fact: In a chat, «asl?» asks for age, sex and location; never answer it, and tell a trusted adult.
exercise: «America has ASL.» What language is ASL? | British Sign Language | *American Sign Language | Ukrainian Spoken English | Auslan
exercise: «BSL fingerspelling uses two hands, ASL fingerspelling uses one.» How many hands does ASL fingerspelling use? | Two | Three | None | *One
exercise: Sam says ASL and BSL are the same language. What is the right answer? | ASL is a type of cake | They both use spoken words only | *They are different languages | They are the same language with two names
test: Why is ASL more than just English words made with the hands? | Because it has no signs | Because it is only used in Australia | Because it is written with letters only | *Because it is a real language with its own grammar
test: A stranger in a chat asks «asl?». What should you do? | Share your address so they know you are nice | Ask them the same question back | *Do not answer, and tell a trusted adult | Give your age, sex and town
exam: In a chat, which clue shows that «asl?» means a personal question and not sign language? | A teacher shows it on a sign language poster | Your friend types it in a school project about hands | *A stranger you do not know asks it in a chat | It appears in a book about Deaf history
